# System Architecture

## Overview

ElectricalDiagramAnalyzer is a Python backend that processes electrical PDF drawings (panel schedules, riser diagrams, one-line diagrams). It detects panel tables via heuristic whitespace/void analysis, extracts structured data (panel name, amperage, voltage, breakers) via OCR, and recommends equipment part numbers through a rules engine. The backend connects to an Anvil web UI via Uplink RPC.

## Component Diagram

```
PDF Upload (Anvil UI)
    │
    ▼
AnvilUplinkCode/uplink_server.py ── vm_submit_for_detection()
    │
    ├─► Page Filtering (one of):
    │   ├── PageFilterV3 (heuristic: EasyOCR + regex + footprints, ~1-3 s/page)
    │   └── PageFilterV4 / PageClassifierML (ML: MobileNetV2 binary classifier, ~250 ms/page)
    │       │  keeps electrical pages, outputs filtered PDF
    │       ▼
    ├─► PanelBoardSearch (PanelSearchToolV26)
    │       │  detects panel regions, crops to PNGs
    │       ▼
    ├─► BreakerTablePipeline (BreakerTableParserAPIv14)
    │       ├─► BreakerTableAnalyzer (Analyzer12)
    │       │       ├── BreakerHeaderFinder
    │       │       └── BreakerFooterFinder
    │       ├─► PanelHeaderParser (PanelHeaderParserV11)
    │       └─► BreakerTableParser (BreakerTableParser12)
    │       │  extracts structured OCR data per panel
    │       ▼
    ├─► _merge_component_from_btp()
    │       │  maps OCR results to component dicts
    │       ▼
    └─► RulesEngine7.process_job()
            └── PartNumberBuilder (mccb, nqPanelboard, Disconnect, ...)
                │  selects equipment part numbers
                ▼
            result.json + status.json on disk
```

### Spec Sheet Analysis Pipeline (separate from detection)

```
Specs PDF Upload (Anvil UI) ── vm_upload_specs_pdf()
    │  saved under ~/jobs/specs/groups/<group>/users/<owner>/specs_<id>/
    ▼
vm_start_specs_analysis() ── background thread ── _run_specs_analysis_job()
    │
    ▼
Spec_Sheet_Analysis/Specs_AnalyzerV5.analyze_specs_pdf_for_ui(pdf_path, job_dir)
    │  text-based section finding, panelboard requirement classification,
    │  evidence-image generation (pypdfium2 text extraction; no OCR/GPU)
    ▼
result.json + status.json on disk; polled via vm_get_specs_status()
```

## Key Components

### AnvilUplinkCode (Orchestrator)
- **Purpose**: Anvil Uplink server; exposes RPC callables, manages job queue, worker pool, and watchdog
- **Location**: `AnvilUplinkCode/uplink_server.py`
- **Dependencies**: PageFilter, PanelBoardSearch, BreakerTablePipeline (APIv13), RulesEngine7, Spec_Sheet_Analysis (Specs_AnalyzerV5), WorkerSetup
- **Consumers**: Anvil web frontend via Uplink RPC
- **Note on active versions**: always confirm the live version by reading the imports at the top of `uplink_server.py` (currently `PageFilterV3`, `PanelSearchToolV26`, `BreakerTableParserAPIv14`, `RulesEngine7`, and `Specs_AnalyzerV5` loaded dynamically).
- **Job recovery (added 2026-08-18, merge `62beba4`)**: stale queued/running jobs are detected via `heartbeat_ts` in `status.json` and requeued automatically after VM/Anvil disconnects (configurable via `STALE_QUEUED_REQUEUE_SEC`, `RECOVERY_MAX_REQUEUE_ATTEMPTS`, etc.). Live progress heartbeats emit incremental finding counts so the UI does not sit at 0% until OCR finishes.

### PageFilter
- **Purpose**: Filters PDF pages to keep only electrical/panel schedule pages
- **Heuristic approach (production)**: `PageFilter/PageFilterV3.py` -- OCR-based E-sheet detection, regex label scoring, whitespace footprints. Slower (~1-3 s/page) due to EasyOCR.
- **Heuristic approach (newer, not yet production)**: `PageFilter/PageFilterV5.py` -- extends the heuristic with index/bookmark-based page skipping for faster runs on bookmarked PDFs. Used in `DevEnv/DevTestEnv.py`; production still imports V3.
- **ML approach**: `PageFilter/PageClassifierML.py` -- MobileNetV2 binary classifier (~250 ms/page on GPU). No OCR needed; classifies entire page images.
- **ML wrapper**: `PageFilter/PageFilterV4.py` -- thin compatibility layer that wraps `PageClassifierML` behind the legacy `PageFilter` class name and `readPdf()` return signature, so callers can swap imports from V3 → V4 without other code changes. All heuristic logic (OCR, regex, footprints) has been removed from V4.
- **Production import**: `uplink_server.py` currently uses `PageFilterV3` (not yet switched to V4 or V5)
- **Dependencies (heuristic)**: pypdfium2, pikepdf, EasyOCR, OpenCV
- **Dependencies (ML)**: pypdfium2, pikepdf, PyTorch, torchvision
- **Consumers**: uplink_server (pre-processing step)

### MLTableDetection
- **Purpose**: ML models for panel schedule detection (DETR-based table detection) and supporting data-prep, training, and evaluation infrastructure. The MobileNetV2 page classifier itself lives in `PageFilter/` (see PageFilter section above); only the (laptop-only, not in-repo) classifier training script ever lived here historically.
- **Location**: `MLTableDetection/`
- **Components**:
  - `TableDetectorML.py` -- DETR-based table detection (Table Transformer / Detectron2 backends). Default model path resolves to the local fine-tuned **v7** checkpoint at `~/Documents/TableAnnotations/models_v7/best/`; falls back to the upstream pretrained `microsoft/table-transformer-detection` if no local checkpoint exists. See `_resolve_default_tatr_model_path()`.
  - `train_table_transformer.py` -- Fine-tuning script for Table Transformer (COCO format). Exposes `--num-queries`, `--freeze-backbone-epochs`, `--hard-negatives-dir`. Production v7 recipe is recorded in `~/Documents/TableAnnotations/baselines/v7_2026-05-15/SUMMARY.md`.
  - `evaluate_model.py` -- mAP evaluation, inference, box-level heuristic comparison.
  - `render_pdfs_for_annotation.py`, `pseudo_label.py`, `synthesize_panels.py`, `merge_annotations.py` -- data-prep utilities.
  - `README.md`, `requirements.txt`, `setup_label_studio.sh` -- module documentation and dev-environment helpers.
- **Dependencies**: PyTorch, torchvision, transformers (`<5`), torchmetrics, albumentations, timm, accelerate.
- **Licenses**: All MIT / BSD-3 / Apache 2.0 (commercial-friendly).
- **Consumers**: `PageFilter/PageClassifierML.py` (page classification, via the V4 wrapper). `TableDetectorML` is **not yet wired into the main pipeline** -- the production table-detection step still uses the heuristic `PanelBoardSearch` (`PanelSearchToolV26`). v7 is ready as a drop-in replacement (same `readPdf()` API as `PanelBoardSearch`, exposed via the `PanelBoardSearch` alias at the bottom of `TableDetectorML.py`), but switching `uplink_server.py` to it is deferred until v7 is validated on a wider customer-PDF set. ML training/eval ground truth remains `PanelSearchToolV25` per the standing decision in `known-issues.mdc`.

### VisualDetectionToolLibrary (Panel Detection)
- **Purpose**: Detects panel table regions in PDF bitmaps using whitespace/void analysis and border detection
- **Location**: `VisualDetectionToolLibrary/PanelSearchToolV26.py` (production). `PanelSearchToolV25.py` is retained as the ML evaluation ground-truth baseline.
- **Dependencies**: pypdfium2, OpenCV, NumPy
- **Consumers**: uplink_server (panel crop step)

### OcrLibrary (OCR Pipeline)
- **Purpose**: Three-stage OCR pipeline -- layout analysis, header parsing, breaker table parsing
- **Location**: `OcrLibrary/` (BreakerTableParserAPIv14, BreakerTableAnalyzer12, PanelHeaderParserV11, BreakerTableParser12)
- **API version string**: `API_VERSION = "API_14"` (exported by `BreakerTableParserAPIv14`)
- **Recent parser hardening (2026-08-18, merge `62beba4`)**: `PanelHeaderParserV11` accepts `BKR`/`BRKR` main-breaker labels and `N/A` as a valid "none" for MAIN/AIC; `BreakerTableParser11` adds breaker-column header synonyms and red review overlays for bad breaker detections (ignoring explicit `" -- "` blank cells).
- **Body-cell grid removal (2026-08-18)**: `BreakerTableParser12` de-grids every breaker body cell via `_prep_cell_for_ocr()` before EasyOCR, which recovers cells whose borders touched the glyphs. Grid lines are still needed *upstream* of this step -- row bands are derived from the horizontal dividers in the raw strip -- so removal happens per row band inside `_ocr_body_column_by_rows()`, never on the strip used for row detection. See the `known-issues.mdc` entry for the measured before/after.
- **Dependencies**: EasyOCR, OpenCV, NumPy, AnchoringClasses
- **Consumers**: uplink_server via BreakerTablePipeline

### AnchoringClasses
- **Purpose**: Structural layout detection -- finds header and footer anchor positions in panel images
- **Location**: `AnchoringClasses/BreakerHeaderFinder.py`, `AnchoringClasses/BreakerFooterFinder.py`
- **Dependencies**: EasyOCR reader (shared), OpenCV, NumPy
- **Consumers**: BreakerTableAnalyzer12

### RulesEngine
- **Purpose**: Processes component dicts from OCR to select equipment part numbers
- **Location**: `RulesEngine/RulesEngine7.py` (imported in `uplink_server.py` as `RE2`; exposes `process_job(payload)`). RulesEngine7 extends RulesEngine6 (MMS/ERMS handling, ground-bar behavior aligned with digest, etc.). RulesEngine6 is retained for comparison.
- **Dependencies**: PartNumberSelector
- **Consumers**: uplink_server (final pipeline step)

### Spec_Sheet_Analysis (Specs Pipeline)
- **Purpose**: Analyzes uploaded specification PDFs (not drawings) to locate the panelboard spec section and classify requirements. Text-extraction based (pypdfium2), not OCR/GPU based, so it runs in a lightweight background thread rather than the GPU worker pool.
- **Location**: `Spec_Sheet_Analysis/Specs_AnalyzerV5.py` -- entry point `analyze_specs_pdf_for_ui(pdf_path, job_dir)`. Loaded dynamically by `uplink_server.py` via `importlib` from the repo path.
- **Dependencies**: pypdfium2, Pillow
- **Consumers**: uplink_server (`_run_specs_analysis_job`, the `vm_*_specs_*` callables)

### WorkerSetup (Runtime Determinism)
- **Purpose**: Centralizes thread-pool caps and determinism settings for spawned workers
- **Location**: `WorkerSetup/worker_init.py` -- `cap_thread_pools()` (sets `OMP_NUM_THREADS`, `MKL_NUM_THREADS`, etc. **before** heavy imports) and `set_runtime_determinism()` (sets torch/OpenCV thread counts and cuDNN determinism **inside** each spawned worker)
- **Dependencies**: None at import (lazily touches torch/cv2 if installed)
- **Consumers**: uplink_server (startup and worker process init)

### PartNumberSelector
- **Purpose**: Part number lookup and selection logic for panelboards, breakers, disconnects, transformers
- **Location**: `PartNumberSelector/PartNumberBuilder.py`
- **Dependencies**: None (pure logic)
- **Consumers**: RulesEngine7

## Data Pipelines

### Main Detection Pipeline
- **Trigger**: `vm_submit_for_detection()` callable from Anvil UI
- **Steps**:
  1. Save uploaded PDF to `~/jobs/{job_id}/`
  2. Write initial `status.json` (state=queued)
  3. Enqueue job to worker pool
  4. Worker spawns subprocess: `PageFilter.readPdf()` filters pages
  5. `PanelBoardSearch.readPdf()` detects and crops panels to PNGs
  6. For each PNG (sequential): `BreakerTablePipeline.run()` extracts OCR data
  7. `_merge_component_from_btp()` maps each OCR result to a component dict (invalidates amperage/voltage/main when the header parser flagged the field missing or the value is out of range; flags parser-problem panels with `_skipped`)
  8. `_rules_safe_components()` sanitizes the component list (see below), then `RulesEngine7.process_job()` selects part numbers for the sanitized components
  9. Write `result.json` and update `status.json` (state=done)
  10. Cleanup temporary files
- **Output**: `result.json` with components, part numbers, and cycle time
- **Error handling**: Exceptions write state=error to `status.json`; watchdog kills jobs exceeding timeout
- **Post-run edits**: After a job completes, the UI can re-run only the rules step without re-doing OCR via `vm_rerun_rules_with_panel_edit()` (edit one component) or `vm_rerun_rules_with_global_defaults()` (re-apply default overrides to all components). Both read `result.json`, re-run RulesEngine, and rewrite `result.json`.

### Quick Compatibility Test Pipeline (added 2026-08-18, merge `62beba4`)
- **Trigger**: `vm_submit_compatibility_test()` callable from Anvil UI
- **Purpose**: Run PageFilter + panel finder + one-panel OCR parse (no RulesEngine, no BOM) so the user can know whether a PDF is likely compatible before submitting a full job
- **Steps**:
  1. Save uploaded PDF to a temporary `compatibility__*` job dir under the grouped per-user layout
  2. Run the same PageFilter configuration as production
  3. Run panel finder; attempt up to `COMPATIBILITY_MAX_PANEL_ATTEMPTS` (default 3) candidate panels through `BreakerTablePipeline`
  4. Write a compatibility report to `result.json`; poll via `vm_get_compatibility_status()`
- **Timeout**: `COMPATIBILITY_TIMEOUT_SEC` (default 60 s); abandoned temp dirs are cleaned up after `COMPATIBILITY_TEMP_RETENTION_MINUTES`
- **Concurrency**: blocked while the user has any active detection job in flight

### Spec Sheet Analysis Pipeline
- **Trigger**: `vm_upload_specs_pdf()` saves the spec PDF, then `vm_start_specs_analysis()` launches a background `threading.Thread` (not the GPU worker pool)
- **Steps**: extract per-page text (pypdfium2) → score/find the panelboard spec section → classify requirement strength → generate evidence images → write `result.json` / `status.json`
- **Polling**: `vm_get_specs_status()` returns state and (when done) the result
- **Storage**: under `~/jobs/specs/groups/<group>/users/<owner>/specs_<id>/`; excluded-from-improvement jobs have their raw PDF deleted post-analysis and artifacts retained only for a short window (`EXCLUDED_SPECS_ARTIFACT_RETENTION_MINUTES`, default 10)

## Deployment Topology

- **Runtime**: Single Paperspace VM
- **Connection**: Anvil Uplink RPC (`anvil.server.connect()` with `ANVIL_UPLINK_KEY`)
- **Concurrency**: 1 worker thread spawning a child process per job (`multiprocessing.spawn`)
- **Per-user limit**: 1 concurrent job per user (`MAX_INFLIGHT_PER_USER`)
- **Watchdog**: Kills child processes exceeding `WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_MIN` (default 10 minutes)
- **Queue timeout**: Jobs waiting longer than `QUEUE_TIMEOUT_MIN` (default 25 minutes) are expired
- **Job storage (grouped)**: Detection jobs live at `~/jobs/groups/<group>/users/<owner>/<job_id>/`; specs jobs at `~/jobs/specs/groups/<group>/users/<owner>/specs_<id>/`. The `group_folder` parameter (default `"personal"`) namespaces a user's jobs. A legacy flat layout (`~/jobs/{job_id}/`) is still resolved as a fallback for old jobs. All client-supplied `job_id` / `group_folder` / `owner_email` values are sanitized and path-traversal-checked before use.
- **Retention/cleanup**: A rolling background cleanup deletes expired excluded jobs (`EXCLUDED_JOB_RETENTION_HOURS`, default 24) and excluded-specs artifacts (`EXCLUDED_SPECS_ARTIFACT_RETENTION_MINUTES`, default 10). Status/result reads opportunistically purge expired excluded jobs.
- **No containerization**: Runs directly on the VM with system Python

## Key Design Decisions

### Process-Based Job Isolation
- **Decision**: Each job runs in a spawned subprocess, not in-thread
- **Context**: EasyOCR, PyTorch, and OpenCV have state that leaks across runs; subprocess ensures clean state
- **Alternatives considered**: Thread-based execution (caused non-deterministic OCR results)
- **Consequences**: Higher per-job overhead but guaranteed determinism and isolation

### Versioned Module Files
- **Decision**: Modules carry version suffixes (e.g., PanelSearchToolV26, BreakerTableAnalyzer12, BreakerTableParserAPIv14)
- **Context**: Enables side-by-side comparison during development; the highest number is *usually* the active version, but there are real exceptions -- `PageFilter` (V3 heuristic is in production; V4 is the ML wrapper; V5 exists but is not wired in), `PanelSearchTool` (V26 is production; V25 remains ML eval ground truth), and `Spec_Sheet_Analysis` (V6 exists but `uplink_server.py` loads **V5**). Active OCR/rules versions as of this writing (2026-08-18): `BreakerTableParserAPIv14`, `PanelHeaderParserV11`, `BreakerTableParser12`, `RulesEngine7`.
- **Alternatives considered**: Git branching only (harder to compare in dev scripts)
- **Consequences**: Multiple version files in each directory; **always check the import in `uplink_server.py` to confirm the active version** rather than assuming the highest number wins

### Dual Page Filtering Paths (Heuristic vs ML)
- **Decision**: ML page classifier (`PageClassifierML`, wrapped by `PageFilterV4`) exists alongside the last heuristic (`PageFilterV3`) as a drop-in replacement
- **Context**: Heuristic pipeline (EasyOCR + regex + footprints) is slow and fragile. ML classifier (MobileNetV2) is faster and more robust but trained on limited data (35 images).
- **Alternatives considered**: Replacing heuristic entirely (premature given small training set)
- **Consequences**: Both paths expose the same `PageFilter` class and `readPdf()` signature. Production can switch by changing the import in `uplink_server.py` from `PageFilter.PageFilterV3` to `PageFilter.PageFilterV4`. The ML classifier requires a trained model checkpoint at `~/Documents/TableAnnotations/models_classifier/best/model.pt`, which is currently present only on Marco's laptop (the Paperspace VM does not have it).

### Local Fine-Tuned Model Default with HF Fallback
- **Decision**: `TableDetectorML` resolves its default TATR model path to a local fine-tuned checkpoint if one exists, otherwise to the upstream HuggingFace pretrained weights. The search order is encoded in `DEFAULT_TATR_LOCAL_PATHS` and is host-aware (`~/Documents/...` is tried first and works on any host via the current user's home; the absolute paths for Paperspace `/home/paperspace/...`, AWS dev box `/home/ubuntu/...`, and Marco's laptop `/home/marco/...` are tried as belt-and-suspenders fallbacks).
- **Context**: The fine-tuned v7 weights live outside the repo at `~/Documents/TableAnnotations/models_v7/best/`. We want production hosts that have the checkpoint to use it as the default, but we also don't want imports to break on hosts that haven't received the weights yet (CI, fresh clones, etc.).
- **Alternatives considered**: (a) Hardcode the local path -- breaks portability; (b) Require an explicit `model_path` argument -- adds friction at every call site; (c) Bake the weights into the repo -- exceeds reasonable repo-size limits (~110 MB per checkpoint).
- **Consequences**: Callers who pass `model_path=None` get the best available model for their host. The fallback to the upstream pretrained model means imports never fail on the resolver step, only the final detection quality degrades to "zero-shot TATR" if no local checkpoint is present. The same pattern can be applied to future ML modules with out-of-repo weights.

### RulesEngine Input Sanitization (Separate from Saved Result)
- **Decision** (added 2026-07-03, merge `04a2361`; still active under RulesEngine7 as of 2026-08-18): every component list is passed through `_rules_safe_components()` in `uplink_server.py` immediately before `RulesEngine7.process_job()`, on all three rules entry points (`_process_job`, `vm_rerun_rules_with_panel_edit`, `vm_rerun_rules_with_global_defaults`). The sanitizer returns a rules-only copy; the persisted `result.json` components still carry the original raw/editable OCR evidence.
- **Context**: Junk or ambiguous OCR (e.g. a "Location: ELECTRICAL 123" string mis-read as bus amps, or a `65k` AIC value read as a breaker amperage) could otherwise flow into the rules engine and manufacture a plausible-looking but wrong quoteable BOM. The fix is defense-in-depth: `PanelHeaderParserV11` now rejects such text at parse time, `_merge_component_from_btp()` invalidates out-of-range/missing header fields, and `_rules_safe_components()` is the final gate right before rules run.
- **Behavior**: problem panels (`_skipped` true or non-empty `panelStatus`) are fully suppressed for rules (all quote-driving attrs nulled, `breaker_data_suppressed`/`breaker_suppression_reason` set) but stay visible/editable in the UI. Clean panels have their values validated/snapped: amps 100-1200 ending in 0/5, voltage in {120,208,240,480,600}, intRating in the standard AIC set, spaces snapped to standard even counts and capped at 84, trim in {SURFACE,FLUSH} (None for NEMA3R), breakers filtered to amps 15-1200 / poles 1-3 / not exceeding the panel limit. Manual edits via `vm_rerun_rules_with_panel_edit` clear stale status flags (`panelStatus`, `panelNote`, `specialHeaderType`, `_skipped`, `reason`, `headerValidation*`) so a user-corrected panel is re-judged purely from the edited attrs.
- **Consequences**: the UI keeps full raw evidence for review/editing, but the BOM logic only ever sees sane inputs. When adding a new quote-driving attribute, extend `_rules_safe_components()` (not just the parser) so it can't leak unvalidated into the rules engine.
